  "response": "Adjudication between rival accounts of the Late Bronze Age collapse begins with a judgement about what each class of evidence is competent to establish, and the Egyptian material is the natural place to begin. The mortuary temple of Ramesses III at Medinet Habu carries reliefs and inscriptions describing a battle against a coalition known as the Sea Peoples. Precisely because that is the coalition which those reliefs and inscriptions describe, it cannot also be the case that the Medinet Habu inscriptions name the Dorians among the members of the Sea Peoples coalition. Although that monumental record presents itself as a plain report of what happened, the Egyptian inscriptions describing the Sea Peoples were composed as royal propaganda celebrating pharaonic victory. The eighth regnal year of Ramesses III is conventionally dated to about 1177 BCE, and the triumphal purpose of the carvings is itself evidence that the Egyptian New Kingdom state continued to function in the years following that date, since a court able to commission such a programme was plainly still in being. Either that continuity obtained or the New Kingdom state was extinguished by the events of about 1177 BCE: one of the two must be the case, and they cannot both be.\n\nThe archives of the Levant and Anatolia pose the same difficulty of genre in another guise. Clay tablets recovered from Ugarit record a ruler's report of enemy ships sighted off the coast. The last letters dispatched from the city before its fall were written in Linear B. A Hittite royal letter requests urgent shipments of grain to relieve shortages; some years afterwards, Hattusa, the capital of the Hittite empire, was destroyed by fire in the early twelfth century BCE. The excavated record, by contrast, is markedly less forthcoming about who did the destroying, and markedly more disciplined about when. Many destruction layers of the period contain no written material identifying the agents of destruction, and that absence has brought it about that radiocarbon dating of destruction deposits supplies a chronology independent of Egyptian regnal records.\n\nPollen cores from the Sea of Galilee indicate a period of reduced rainfall beginning around 1250 BCE, and that signal confirms that a prolonged drought affected the eastern Mediterranean in the decades around 1200 BCE. Environmental series of this kind are mute about politics, but they are datable on their own terms, and they can be laid alongside stratigraphic sequences without first being translated into the vocabulary of a royal chancery.\n\nThe practical method, then, is neither to privilege the texts nor to treat them as mere embellishment, but to ask of each body of material what kind of question it can answer. Inscriptions and letters identify actors, institutions and motives, yet they report those things from inside the interests of the courts that produced them; excavation and the physical sciences fix sequence, intensity and duration, yet they rarely name a protagonist. Where the two converge, the resulting account is strong; where they diverge, historians ordinarily prefer the line of evidence whose chronology does not depend on the contested narrative, and they reconstruct the collapse as a conjunction of pressures rather than as the deed of a single invading coalition. Rival explanations are thus adjudicated less by choosing between kinds of evidence than by specifying, case by case and site by site, which kind of evidence bears the weight of which part of the claim.",
